# What to return

- `mini_summary` (English, one sentence, at most 250 characters): what the episode covered, concrete enough that someone who sees only this line can tell whether a new topic relates to it. Name the core paper, method or phenomenon, not just the field.
- `reference_summary` (English Markdown, 200 to 400 words), for a script writer who wants to refer back:
  - the core claims and the key numbers, as they were said in the episode;
  - which concepts were explained and **how** (the analogies and examples used), so a later episode can call them back instead of explaining again;
  - terms that were defined;
  - limits, controversies and questions the episode left open.
  Only what the episode actually said; nothing new.
- `key_concepts`: 3 to 10 central concepts or methods, short noun phrases in English.
- `follow_ups`: up to {{ max_follow_ups }} follow-up episodes, the most rewarding first. Good follow-ups go deeper into something the episode only touched, explain a foundation it relied on, cover the critique or a competing approach, or follow the newest work. Each must be a substantial episode of its own{% if library %}, and must not repeat an earlier episode (building on one is fine){% endif %}. In {{ language_name }}:
  - `title`: a working title, at most 60 characters;
  - `topic`: the request for the new episode, one to three sentences that make sense without this episode (name papers, methods or authors where you can);
  - `why`: one sentence on what the listener gains and how it builds on this episode.{% if series %}
  Make the **first** suggestion the natural next part of the series "{{ series.title }}", following its common thread; the others may branch off.{% endif %}
